Article 21

Services for women victims of violence may be carried out through:

a. receiving reports and/or outreach to victims of violence;
b. providing information about the rights of victims of violence; c. facilitating the provision of health services;
d. facilitating the provision of psychological strengthening services; e. facilitating the provision of psychosocial services, social rehabilitation, social empowerment, repatriation, and social reintegration;
f. provision of legal services;
g. identification of economic empowerment needs; h. identification of temporary shelter needs for victims of violence and families of victims of violence that need to be met immediately;
i. facilitating the needs of victims of violence with disabilities;
j. coordination and cooperation on the fulfillment of the rights of victims of violence with other institutions; and
k. monitoring the fulfillment of the rights of victims of violence by law enforcement apparatus during the judicial proceedings.

Article 18

(1) The implementation of Women's Protection through: a. prevention of violence against women; b. services for women victims of violence; and

c. strengthening Women's Protection institutions.

(2) The implementation of Women's Protection as referred to in Article 18 may be provided to vulnerable women groups.

(3) Vulnerable women groups as referred to in paragraph (2) include:

a. poor women;
b. women with disabilities; c. women with HIV/AIDS;
d. migrant women workers;
e. elderly women;
f. indigenous women;
g. women in disaster and social conflict situations; h. female heads of households;
i. informal women workers;
j. women in situations of intolerance;
k. women victims/exposed to NAPZA (narcotics, psychotropics, and addictive substances);
l. women victims/exposed to terrorism, radicalism and extremism; and/or m.other vulnerable women groups.

Article 22

(1) Institutional strengthening of Women's Protection may be carried out through capacity building, cooperation and integration with:

a. government agencies;
b. educational institutions;
c. religious institutions;
d. health service provider institutions; e. professional institutions;
f. business sectors;
g. service institutions;
h. Islamic boarding schools;
i. youth organizations;
j. legal aid organizations;
k. media;
l. religious leaders;
m. community leaders;
n. communities; and
o. families.
(2) Strengthening and development of Women's Protection service provider institutions shall be implemented through: a. capacity building of human resources of Women's Protection service provider institutions for victims of violence at the Regional level;
b. provision of specific needs for women in emergency situations and special conditions at the Regional level;
c. development of communication, information, and education strategies on the protection of women's rights at the Regional level;
d. strengthening networks among Women's Protection service provider institutions at the Regional level;
e. facilitating efforts to meet the standards of Women's Protection service provider institutions at the Regional level; f. development of Women's Protection service provider institutions at the Regional level.

Article 19

(1) Prevention of Violence Against Women as referred to in Article 18 letter a may be carried out in the following forms:

a. developing communication media, information, education and public campaigns;
b. developing educational materials and curriculum; c. developing safe transportation systems and public spaces;
d. establishing early detection systems, security and integrated complaint services in industrial areas, companies, residential areas, educational environments, Islamic boarding schools and other public spaces;
e. providing consultation, information, education, and counseling services;
f. developing policies related to safe and friendly schools/madrasahs, boarding schools, Islamic boarding schools, and higher education institutions;
g. establishing and developing cadres, communities, and groups from among youth, students, Islamic students, university students, journalists, social media influencers, educators, and religious leaders;
h. conducting education and advocacy to owners, managers and/or social media users;
i. developing protection systems and special support for vulnerable women groups;
j. conducting awareness raising for perpetrators;
k. increasing the capacity of regional governments, government agencies, institutions, community institutions, business sectors, Islamic boarding schools, community organizations, media, religious leaders, community leaders and communities;
l. conducting cooperation with religious institutions, community social institutions, service institutions, higher education institutions, Islamic boarding schools, media, youth organizations, religious leaders and community leaders; and
m. conducting socialization of laws and regulations.
(2) Forms of prevention of Violence Against Women as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be implemented in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.

Article 3

Empowerment and Protection of Women aims to:

a. improve the quality of life of women;
b. increase the participation of women in the development process;
c. encourage women's leadership and women's bargaining position in decision-making in various development processes, both planning, implementation and monitoring and evaluation;
d. increase the ability of women to manage the economy, both in meeting household needs and in opening productive and independent employment opportunities;
e. provide protection of women's rights from various forms of violence and other discriminatory treatment;
f. prevent all forms of Violence Against Women;
g. provide services and recovery for women victims of violence.
h. increase community participation in the Protection of Women;
i. realize a social life that is safe and free from all forms of Violence Against Women; and
j. increase the strengthening and development of institutions providing Women's Empowerment services.

Article 20

Women victims of violence shall receive the following rights:

a. the right to have their dignity and worth as human beings respected;
b. the right to recovery;
c. the right to determine their own decisions;
d. the right to easy access to information; e. the right to confidentiality;
f. the right to social rehabilitation;
g. the right to complaint handling that is fast, appropriate, comfortable and according to needs;
h. the right of victims and their families to receive facilitation in the judicial process;
i. the right to assistance; and
j. the right to feel secure.

